book.create_kiday.chronicle.page_1:
There lived a smith whose skill was known afar;
They called him Kitay Bahsi, proud of his art.

He found a rare ore in the earth's deep breast;
Its hue, its form, its very being held secrets.

book.create_kiday.chronicle.page_2:
He struck with many hammers, yet iron took no shape;
His anvil weary, his arm spent, the ore endured.

At last he fired it, poured it in a mold;
In water it found its shape—a wondrous secret revealed.

book.create_kiday.chronicle.page_3:
He forged armor and tools, and saw their might;
Each held a power hidden from the light.

He named it Kiday, among all craftsmen;
From smith to smith, it passed—a keepsake.

book.create_kiday.chronicle.page_4:
One night he watched the lightning cross the sky;
The thought that came would shape the world.

He joined heaven's fire to an iron wheel's axle;
Each bolt from above would drive its turning.

book.create_kiday.chronicle.page_5:
Proud Lei Gong heard of this craft and raged;
A mortal's skill brought shame to him.

He sent his likenesses to every corner of the earth;
Yet each was powerless there, without might.

book.create_kiday.chronicle.page_6:
He commanded clouds; the rain would never cease;
The storm-ruled groves lay ravaged.

Many ages passed; the tree took another form;
Human faces appeared upon each branch's fruit.

book.create_kiday.chronicle.page_7:
The yellow fruits called out, “vak vak”;
Within each, a hidden gift of mystery lay.

Those who ate its fruit could no longer be struck by lightning;
The proud one's wrath became a shield for humankind.
